# Shell scripts must be LF in the working tree, not just in the repository.
#
# This is not a tidiness rule. The dev box is Windows, so git checks files out
# with CRLF by default; scripts/test_docker.sh then bind-mounts this checkout
# into a Linux container, where bash reads the CR as part of each command and
# fails with "$'\r': command not found". The script is unrunnable on the very
# platform it exists to serve. build.sh has the same problem from WSL.
#
# Forcing eol=lf fixes both, and costs nothing on Windows: every editor in play
# here (and PowerShell) handles LF fine.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for anything else that a Linux shell or interpreter may execute
# directly from a mounted checkout.
*.py text eol=lf
